{
 "version": 3,
 "meta": {
  "name": "polish_strip_gt",
  "description": "Polish three marks off the strip against the fixed spinning drum, bimanually, with ground truth. Dual pick off the mat, then BOTH hands hold the strip from there to the set-down: each mark is brought to the drum by shifting the whole rigid body 33 mm, hands travelling with it, because a single hand -- which holds the strip perfectly well -- cannot react the press torque: the mark is 78 mm from the grasp and the grip's couple is only as wide as the strip is thick. The gated close arms a force hold on the fingers, without which the grip bleeds from 40 N to nothing over three marks and the strip ends up on the bench. The drum is clamped SIDEWAYS, so it works on its barrel and its contact is a narrow band rather than a disc: every mark is pressed and then RUBBED back and forth ALONG the spindle axis, three cycles a round and up to three rounds, polled between rounds by sim.wait_polish's feedback dwell. The direction is not a choice. The drum's surface runs across its axis -- along the strip's own long axis -- so a rub that way works against the full mu*press drag in the one direction an end-on pinch cannot hold; a rub along the axis is perpendicular to it and nearly free. Shifts BETWEEN marks are still lift-and-shift, never an in-contact drag."
 },
